{
  "script": [
    {
      "beatId": "b1",
      "narration": "At Larkspur Pay, checkout failed for forty-seven minutes. Thirty-one percent of payment requests errored. Why did a store failover break checkout?",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Slow store broke checkout",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Checkout errors",
        "Store failover"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "31%",
          "label": "checkout requests failed",
          "sourceRef": "num-19"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay post-incident report, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b2",
      "narration": "So a rate limiter decides which requests pass now. It uses a token bucket, permission tokens refilled at a set rate. Its cap is one hundred tokens.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Token bucket = permission tokens",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Token bucket"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "100 tokens",
          "label": "bucket capacity",
          "sourceRef": "num-1"
        }
      ],
      "source": "A field guide to token-bucket rate limiting"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b3",
      "narration": "The refill is computed, not scheduled: elapsed seconds times the refill rate, ten tokens a second. The shopper's checkout spends one token. An empty bucket answers four-two-nine, with a wait hint.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Refill computed, not scheduled",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Refused",
        "Retry-After"
      ],
      "source": "A field guide to token-bucket rate limiting"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b4",
      "narration": "But Hollowmere runs twenty-four gateway nodes. A bucket per node gives a client twenty-four times its limit. So the buckets live in one shared store, seen by every node.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "One shared store, one limit",
        "semantic": "decision"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Shared store"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "24 nodes",
          "label": "behind the load balancer",
          "sourceRef": "num-3"
        }
      ],
      "source": "A field guide to token-bucket rate limiting"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b5",
      "narration": "Here's the catch: two nodes read the same count, both see one token, and both let a request through. So the store runs read, refill, spend in one all-or-nothing step.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Read, refill, spend: one step",
        "semantic": "decision"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Atomic step",
        "Last token"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b6",
      "narration": "Then, in March twenty twenty-five, Larkspur's checkout limiter store failed over to another zone. Store calls jumped from about one millisecond. They now took eighty. Past the fifty-millisecond timeout, each call counted as a failure.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Slow store trips the timeout",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Replica failover",
        "Store timeout"
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay post-incident report, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b7",
      "narration": "Larkspur had chosen fail open: let traffic through when the store cannot count it. So each of the forty instances enforced the full limit alone. Downstream traffic hit forty times the contracted rate.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Fail open multiplies the limit",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Fail open",
        "Local limits"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "40 times",
          "label": "over the contract",
          "sourceRef": "num-23"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay post-incident report, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b8",
      "narration": "So the shopper's checkout is refused, not overspent. Larkspur made that limiter fail closed and split local limits by instance count. The store timeout went to one hundred fifty milliseconds.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Refuse instead of overspend",
        "semantic": "resolution"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Fail closed",
        "Divided limits",
        "150 ms timeout"
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay post-incident report, 14 March 2025"
    }
  ]
}

